# Task 2 — Secured and Monitored Web Infrastructure

## Infrastructure overview

Same three-server infrastructure as Task 1, now hardened with firewalls,
HTTPS encryption via an SSL certificate, and monitoring agents on every node.

---

## Why each additional element?

- **3 Firewalls**: one in front of the load balancer and one per backend
  server. They filter traffic and block unauthorized ports, reducing the attack
  surface of every machine.
- **SSL certificate**: enables HTTPS so traffic between the user's browser and
  the infrastructure is encrypted. Without it, passwords, cookies, and
  personal data travel in plain text.
- **3 Monitoring clients**: one agent per server (load balancer + 2 servers).
  They collect metrics and logs and ship them to a central platform (Sumo
  Logic) so the team can detect and react to incidents.

---

## What are firewalls for?

A firewall inspects incoming and outgoing network packets and applies rules to
allow or block them. For example, only ports 80 (HTTP) and 443 (HTTPS) are
open to the public. All other ports are blocked, preventing unauthorized
access to SSH, MySQL, or internal services.

---

## Why serve traffic over HTTPS?

HTTPS uses TLS to encrypt the connection between the user and the server.
This prevents man-in-the-middle attacks where an attacker on the same network
could read or modify the data in transit. It also provides authentication
(the SSL certificate proves the server is the legitimate foobar.com) and is
required for browser trust indicators and SEO ranking.

---

## What is monitoring used for?

Monitoring tracks the health, performance, and availability of every component
in real time. It enables the team to detect crashes, high CPU usage, slow
response times, or abnormal traffic patterns, and to receive alerts before
users notice a problem.

---

## How does the monitoring tool collect data?

A lightweight agent (e.g. Sumo Logic collector) runs as a background process
on each server. It reads system metrics (CPU, RAM, disk), tails log files
(Nginx access/error logs, application logs), and ships the data to the Sumo
Logic cloud platform over HTTPS at regular intervals.

---

## How to monitor Nginx QPS (Queries Per Second)?

Enable the Nginx stub_status module by adding this to the Nginx config:

    location /nginx_status {
        stub_status on;
        allow 127.0.0.1;
        deny all;
    }

Then configure the monitoring agent to scrape that endpoint and parse the
"requests" counter. In Sumo Logic, create a metric that calculates the rate
of change of that counter per second to get the QPS. Set an alert if QPS
exceeds a defined threshold.

---

## Issues with this infrastructure

### SSL termination at the load balancer level
When HAProxy decrypts HTTPS and forwards plain HTTP to the backend servers,
the internal traffic between the load balancer and the servers is unencrypted.
If the internal network is compromised, an attacker can read all data. True
end-to-end encryption requires HTTPS all the way to the backend servers.

### Single MySQL Primary for writes
Only the Primary node can accept write operations. If the Primary goes down,
all write queries fail and the application breaks for any action that modifies
data (login, form submission, order creation, etc.). It is a SPOF for the
write path.

### All servers run the same components (DB + App + Web)
Mixing the database, application server, and web server on the same machine
makes each component compete for the same CPU and RAM. It also means a
misconfiguration or crash in one component can affect all others. Separating
concerns onto dedicated servers allows each layer to be scaled, secured, and
maintained independently.
